//! lotus/silence_span.rye — ALES110: silence_span — write silence over `count` samples starting at `start`, IN PLACE, leaving the length and every sample outside the span
//! exactly as they were. This is the third and last way a span meets silence, beside ALES2's cut (which removes a span and SHRINKS the clip) and ALES109's insert_silence
//! (which opens a gap and GROWS the clip): silence_span OVERWRITES the span and leaves the length exactly as it is. This is the Silence Audio Selection button every editor
//! ships — drop a cough, mute a bleed, clear a region to quiet without moving anything around it. Naming it beside cut and insert_silence completes the trio and teaches the
//! one distinction that matters about a span of quiet: REMOVE, OPEN, or OVERWRITE.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: silence_span(clip, start, count) writes zero into the count samples starting at start, leaving every sample outside the span and the length itself
//! exactly as they were. It refuses BadRange when the span falls outside the current samples — checked without underflow, count against the remainder — before any write,
//! reusing ALES2's EditError whole. One in-place pass bounded by count, no allocation, no second buffer.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE SILENCE LAW: after silence_span(clip, start, count) the span [start, start+count) is all zero and every sample outside it is byte-for-byte
//! unchanged, the length untouched, checked against a hand-built vector. THE TRIO / LENGTH LAW: the length is exactly unchanged — the third way a span meets silence beside
//! cut (which shrinks by count) and insert_silence (which grows by count), the same span proven all three ways in one selftest. THE IDEMPOTENCE / PEAK LAW: silence_span is
//! IDEMPOTENT (silencing a span twice equals once) and NOT invertible (the overwritten samples are gone, a lossy overwrite honest about its loss); the peak re-measured
//! through ALES13 is non-increasing — silencing the span that holds the loudest sample strictly lowers the peak, silencing an already-silent span holds it. THE DEGENERATE /
//! REFUSAL LAW: count = 0 the identity, silence_span(0, len) clears the whole clip, the empty clip its own silence, a start past the samples or a count past the remainder
//! refuses BadRange before any write.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES110.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-010618_fill-ales110-lotus-silence-span.md
//! Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, its buf/len/samples, the EditError reused whole, and the cut imported by the selftest to prove the length trio against
//! ALES109's insert_silence; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) — the peak/RMS meter, used only by the witness to prove that overwriting with silence never raises the peak.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. A bounded in-process buffer of i16 PCM on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It writes SILENCE (zero) over an existing span — the
//! one thing it writes, and silence is the honest content of a region a keeper cleared on purpose; it invents no non-zero sample, moves nothing, changes no length, and reads
//! no byte past len. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/silence_span.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");
const insert_silence = @import("insert_silence.rye");

pub const silence_span_version = "20260815.010618";

/// silence_span forwards ALES2's OWN fault reused by name — BadRange when the span falls outside the current samples — and invents none of its own; `count` is a plain span
/// length with no illegal value save the range the samples already name.
pub const SilenceSpanError = timeline.EditError;

/// Write silence over the `count` samples starting at `start`, in place — the length and every sample outside the span left exactly as they were. Refuses BadRange when the
/// span falls outside the current samples, before any write. This is ALES2's cut read as an overwrite rather than a removal — the length-preserving third of the trio.
pub fn silence_span(clip: *timeline.Clip, start: u32, count: u32) SilenceSpanError!void {
    // invariant: the length never claims more samples than the buffer holds — every seat we clear is inside the buffer.
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);

    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples (checked without underflow — count against the remainder), refused by name before any write.
    if (start > clip.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip.len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const end: u32 = start + count;
    // invariant: the span end is within the samples, so every seat cleared below is a legal sample.
    assert(end <= clip.len);

    var i: u32 = start;
    while (i < end) : (i += 1) clip.buf[i] = 0;

    // invariant: silence_span writes values only — the length and its bound are exactly what they were.
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE SILENCE LAW — silence_span zeroes exactly the named span and leaves the rest byte for byte; THE TRIO / LENGTH LAW — the same span keeps the length here,
//     shrinks it through ALES2's cut, and grows it through ALES109's insert_silence; THE IDEMPOTENCE / PEAK LAW — silencing twice equals once, and the peak re-measured
//     through ALES13 is non-increasing (silencing the loudest span strictly lowers it, an already-silent span holds); THE DEGENERATE / REFUSAL LAW — count 0 the identity,
//     silence_span(0, len) all silence, the empty clip its own silence, a span past the samples BadRange ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

/// The clip's current peak magnitude, re-measured through ALES13 — the reader the peak law checks the result against.
fn clip_peak(clip: *const timeline.Clip) !u32 {
    const m = try meter.measure(clip);
    return meter.peak(&m);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE SILENCE LAW — silence_span zeroes exactly the named span and leaves every sample outside it, the length untouched. Proven at a mid span, the head, and the tail.
    {
        var mid = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&mid, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        try silence_span(&mid, 1, 3); // clear samples 1,2,3
        if (!eq(mid.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 0, 0, 0, 50 })) return error.SilenceMidWrong;

        var head = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&head, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30 });
        try silence_span(&head, 0, 2); // clear the head
        if (!eq(head.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 30 })) return error.SilenceHeadWrong;

        var tail = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&tail, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30 });
        try silence_span(&tail, 1, 2); // clear through the tail
        if (!eq(tail.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 0, 0 })) return error.SilenceTailWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE TRIO / LENGTH LAW — the same span [1, 1+2) meets silence three ways: silence_span KEEPS the length (overwrite), ALES2's cut SHRINKS it by 2 (remove), and
    //    ALES109's insert_silence GROWS it by 2 (open). One span, three lengths, proven side by side.
    {
        const src = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };

        var over = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&over, &src);
        try silence_span(&over, 1, 2);
        if (over.len != src.len) return error.OverwriteChangedLength; // kept
        if (!eq(over.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 0, 0, 40 })) return error.OverwriteWrong;

        var removed = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&removed, &src);
        try timeline.cut(&removed, 1, 2);
        if (removed.len != src.len - 2) return error.CutLengthWrong; // shrank by 2
        if (!eq(removed.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 40 })) return error.CutWrong;

        var opened = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&opened, &src);
        try insert_silence.insert_silence(&opened, 1, 2);
        if (opened.len != src.len + 2) return error.InsertLengthWrong; // grew by 2
        if (!eq(opened.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 0, 0, 20, 30, 40 })) return error.InsertWrong;
    }

    // 3. THE IDEMPOTENCE / PEAK LAW — silencing a span twice equals once, and the peak re-measured through ALES13 is non-increasing: silencing the span that holds the
    //    loudest sample strictly lowers the peak, silencing an already-silent span holds it.
    {
        var idem = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&idem, &[_]i16{ 5, 6, 7, 8 });
        try silence_span(&idem, 1, 2);
        const once = [_]i16{ 5, 0, 0, 8 };
        if (!eq(idem.samples(), &once)) return error.IdempotenceFirstWrong;
        try silence_span(&idem, 1, 2); // again — no further change
        if (!eq(idem.samples(), &once)) return error.IdempotenceSecondWrong;

        var drop_peak = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&drop_peak, &[_]i16{ 100, -30000, 200, -50 });
        const before = try clip_peak(&drop_peak);
        try silence_span(&drop_peak, 1, 1); // clear the loudest sample
        const after = try clip_peak(&drop_peak);
        if (!(after < before)) return error.PeakShouldHaveFallen;

        var keep_peak = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&keep_peak, &[_]i16{ -30000, 0, 0, 100 });
        const kb = try clip_peak(&keep_peak);
        try silence_span(&keep_peak, 1, 2); // clear an already-silent span — the loud -30000 stays
        if (try clip_peak(&keep_peak) != kb) return error.KeepPeakMoved;
    }

    // 4a. THE DEGENERATE LAW — count 0 is the identity (nothing clears), silence_span(0, len) clears the whole clip, and the empty clip is its own silence.
    {
        var idc = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&idc, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 });
        try silence_span(&idc, 1, 0); // zero count — the identity
        if (!eq(idc.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 })) return error.ZeroCountMoved;

        var whole = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&whole, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 });
        try silence_span(&whole, 0, whole.len); // clear the whole clip
        if (!eq(whole.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 })) return error.WholeNotSilenced;

        var empty = timeline.Clip{};
        try silence_span(&empty, 0, 0); // empty clip, empty span — its own silence
        if (empty.len != 0) return error.EmptyChanged;
    }

    // 4b. THE REFUSAL LAW — a `start` past the current samples and a `count` past the remainder each refuse BadRange before any write, the clip untouched.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        if (silence_span(&clip, 4, 1)) |_| return error.StartNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (silence_span(&clip, 2, 5)) |_| return error.CountNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        // The clip is untouched by the refused calls — each refusal came before any write.
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.RefusedCallWrote;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-silence-span: SILENCE_SPAN — write silence over `count` samples starting at `start`, IN PLACE, leaving the length and every sample outside the span " ++
        "exactly as they were, the third way a span meets silence beside ALES2's cut (which SHRINKS the clip) and ALES109's insert_silence (which GROWS it): silence_span " ++
        "OVERWRITES and keeps the length. This is the Silence Audio Selection button every editor ships — drop a cough, mute a bleed, clear a region to quiet without moving " ++
        "anything around it. The rule: zero [start, start+count) in one bounded pass, refusing BadRange when the span falls outside the samples before any write; no second " ++
        "buffer. The crux is four laws. THE SILENCE LAW: the span goes all zero and the rest stays byte for byte, the length untouched. THE TRIO / LENGTH LAW: the same span " ++
        "keeps the length here, shrinks it through cut, and grows it through insert_silence. THE IDEMPOTENCE / PEAK LAW: silencing twice equals once, and the peak " ++
        "re-measured through ALES13 is non-increasing (the loudest span strictly lowers it, an already-silent span holds). THE DEGENERATE / REFUSAL LAW: count 0 the " ++
        "identity, silence_span(0, len) all silence, the empty clip its own silence, a span past the samples BadRange. Purely local — it writes silence over an existing " ++
        "span, inventing no non-zero sample, moving nothing, changing no length, reading no byte past len; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real " ++
        "speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
